# 5. SCHOOL MANAGEMENT SYSTEM

Build the management system as a separate, securely authenticated application area.

It should share the school's visual identity but use a practical, data-oriented interface.

Do not create a decorative dashboard that merely resembles functioning software.

## Initial user roles

### Administrator
- Manage authorised user accounts.
- Create and update student records.
- Organise classes and class lists.
- Manage attendance records.
- View and generate authorised academic reports.
- Review teacher activity reports.
- Publish announcements.
- Manage appropriate access permissions.

### Teacher
- View only the classes and students they are authorised to access.
- Record student attendance.
- Enter permitted academic results.
- Submit teacher activity reports.
- View relevant class information.
- Read school announcements.

### Parent — optional future phase
- Secure access to their own child's records only.
- View authorised attendance and results.
- Read school announcements.

Do not implement parent access as unrestricted access to the student database.

## Core modules

### A. Authentication and authorisation
Implement real authentication through an established authentication provider or a properly secured backend.

Requirements:

- Secure sign-in and sign-out.
- Secure session management.
- Role-based access control.
- Server-side permission checks.
- Password reset or recovery through a supported secure process.
- Rate limiting or equivalent protections where appropriate.
- No hard-coded administrator credentials.
- No public registration for privileged roles unless explicitly authorised.

Hiding a button in the frontend is not a security boundary. All sensitive operations must enforce permissions on the server.

### B. Student records
Store the minimum information needed for school operations.

Possible fields:

- Student identifier.
- Student name.
- Class and section.
- Admission information.
- Parent or guardian contact details where necessary.
- Active or inactive status.

Collect additional personal information only where required and authorised.

### C. Attendance
Support:

- Daily attendance.
- Class-based attendance entry.
- Present, absent and other school-approved attendance statuses.
- Editing permissions.
- Attendance history.
- Date-based reports.
- Duplicate-entry prevention.
- Audit history for important changes.

### D. Academic results
Support:

- Class and student selection.
- Subject configuration based on verified school requirements.
- Assessment records.
- Marks and grades according to approved rules.
- Student performance summaries.
- Printable or exportable reports where authorised.

Do not hard-code unverified grading systems.

### E. Teacher activity reports
Support:

- Teacher identity.
- Report date or reporting period.
- Class or activity reference where appropriate.
- Activities completed.
- Notes or follow-up items.
- Submission status.
- Administrator review where required.

Keep performance assessment fair, transparent and based on clearly defined criteria. Do not invent automated teacher rankings.

### F. Announcements
Allow authorised administrators to create, edit and publish school announcements.

Implement actual persistence and appropriate access controls.

## Database and backend

Inspect the existing architecture before selecting or replacing the backend.

If a suitable backend already exists, reuse it where safe.

If no suitable backend exists, propose a practical solution using a managed backend and relational database, such as PostgreSQL with an appropriate authentication and access-control layer.

Possible options include Supabase or a properly implemented custom backend.

Before implementation:

- Identify the existing database and authentication system, if any.
- Document the schema.
- Define role permissions.
- Plan data migrations.
- Use environment variables and server-side secret storage.
- Configure production access policies.
- Add appropriate backups and recovery procedures.
- Document setup and deployment.

If Supabase is used, configure row-level security and policies for every sensitive table. Do not rely solely on frontend filtering.

If a feature cannot be completed because credentials, external services, or configuration are missing, implement the safe parts and document the exact remaining requirements.

Do not claim the portal is production-ready until security and functionality have been tested.

